WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.372
Theo passes the exam, pays the 54 dollar

00:00:03.372 --> 00:00:06.323
fee and receives his license in January.

00:00:06.323 --> 00:00:11.381
In February a carrier he has never worked with accepts a health

00:00:11.381 --> 00:00:16.439
application he solicited; it must appoint him or reject the application within

00:00:16.439 --> 00:00:21.497
30 days, and it appoints him, sending written documentation within 30 days.

00:00:21.497 --> 00:00:26.133
In April he moves and updates his address within 30 days.

00:00:26.133 --> 00:00:31.613
In October he is charged with a misdemeanor unrelated to driving and reports

00:00:31.613 --> 00:00:37.092
it within 30 days of his first court appearance, attaching the charging document.

00:00:37.092 --> 00:00:42.572
Every clock in his first year is 30 days except the one he

00:00:42.572 --> 00:00:47.630
must not confuse: continuing education, due 15 days before his license expires.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:51.760
In March a client pays a 1,200 dollar annual premium in cash.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.858
Theo deposits it in AMG’s premium account, never the

00:00:54.858 --> 00:00:57.612
operating account, and remits it to the carrier.

00:00:57.612 --> 00:01:01.053
A different carrier pays no commission on a small plan,

00:01:01.053 --> 00:01:04.495
so Theo charges a placement fee; the law caps it

00:01:04.495 --> 00:01:07.937
at 15 percent of the premium, 180 dollars on 1,200.

00:01:07.937 --> 00:01:11.035
A prospect hesitates, and Theo is tempted to offer

00:01:11.035 --> 00:01:14.133
to pay her first month; that would be rebating.

00:01:14.133 --> 00:01:18.951
Instead he gives her the same 20 dollar planning guide he gives everyone, which

00:01:18.951 --> 00:01:23.770
is lawful because it costs under 50 dollars and is not conditioned on buying.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:26.812
A competitor’s client asks Theo to compare plans.

00:01:26.812 --> 00:01:30.994
Theo lays out both plans accurately, including the competitor’s broader network

00:01:30.994 --> 00:01:34.796
and his own plan’s lower premium, and documents the comparison.

00:01:34.796 --> 00:01:39.739
That is a fair presentation, and if the client switches, a lawful replacement.

00:01:39.739 --> 00:01:43.921
He does not tell her the competitor is in financial trouble,

00:01:43.921 --> 00:01:48.103
which would be defamation if false, and he does not hide

00:01:48.103 --> 00:01:51.905
his plan’s referral requirement, which would make the comparison twisting.

00:01:51.905 --> 00:01:56.468
He also knows that a false claim in a mailer would be

00:01:56.468 --> 00:02:00.650
false advertising, for which his insurer and he would both answer.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.571
Priya’s bakery, with 18 average employees, is a small

00:02:03.571 --> 00:02:06.167
employer and enjoys guaranteed availability and community rating.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:10.710
One baker’s 24-year-old married son is covered: Maryland requires coverage of unmarried children to

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:14.928
25, but the federal rule to 26 regardless of marriage governs her plan.

00:02:14.928 --> 00:02:18.174
Another baker has a child on January 10; the baby

00:02:18.174 --> 00:02:21.419
is covered from birth, and because the plan charges per

00:02:21.419 --> 00:02:24.339
child, notice and premium are due within 31 days.

00:02:24.339 --> 00:02:27.584
A third baker is ordered by a court to cover

00:02:27.584 --> 00:02:30.829
a child who lives with the other parent; the carrier

00:02:30.829 --> 00:02:33.750
enrolls the child at once, outside any enrollment period.

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:37.426
Dennis enrolls in Part B in March at 65.

00:02:37.426 --> 00:02:42.735
His six-month Medigap open enrollment runs through August; the Plan G policy Theo

00:02:42.735 --> 00:02:47.637
delivers on April 10 carries a 30-day return right through May 10.

00:02:47.637 --> 00:02:52.538
Next year, in the 30 days after his birthday, he may switch

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:57.439
to an equal or lesser plan without underwriting under Maryland’s birthday rule.

00:02:57.439 --> 00:02:59.890
Gloria, 71, considers long-term care insurance.

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:05.199
Theo gives her the consumer guide, the outline of coverage and the personal

00:03:05.199 --> 00:03:10.509
worksheet, the insurer offers 5 percent compound inflation protection and a nonforfeiture benefit,

00:03:10.509 --> 00:03:15.410
and her 3,000 dollar premium earns a 500 dollar Maryland tax credit.

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.044
In December a former client complains to the

00:03:18.044 --> 00:03:20.348
Administration that Theo misdescribed a limited policy.

00:03:20.348 --> 00:03:23.969
The Commissioner sends notice of a proposed penalty; Theo demands a

00:03:23.969 --> 00:03:27.261
hearing in writing, and it is held within 30 days.

00:03:27.261 --> 00:03:30.224
His documented sales presentation, outline of coverage and delivery

00:03:30.224 --> 00:03:33.187
receipt show what he said and gave the client.

00:03:33.187 --> 00:03:34.504
The complaint is dismissed.

00:03:34.504 --> 00:03:38.125
Had the facts been otherwise, the penalty could have run from

00:03:38.125 --> 00:03:41.746
100 to 5,000 dollars per violation with restitution, and a revocation

00:03:41.746 --> 00:03:45.038
would have left him 30 days to seek judicial review.

00:03:45.038 --> 00:03:48.330
Records, candor and calendars are the whole of Maryland compliance.
